 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Per-ray register accumulation for volume rendering
-A forward pass optimization where RGB, depth, and opacity are accumulated in per-thread registers (stack-allocated) rather than global memory, writing each per-ray output exactly once to eliminate per-sample global-memory read-modify-write operations [S2, unofficial]. It differs from the baseline by eliminating per-sample global-memory read-modify-write on `outRGB`/`outDepth`/`outOpacity`, reducing global memory traffic [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when minimizing global memory bandwidth is critical for performance.
+A forward pass optimization where RGB, depth, and opacity are accumulated in per-thread registers (stack-allocated) rather than global memory, writing each per-ray output exactly once to eliminate per-sample global-memory read-modify-write operations [S2, unofficial]. It differs from the baseline by eliminating per-sample global-memory read-modify-write on `outRGB`/`outDepth`/`outOpacity`, reducing global memory traffic [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when global memory bandwidth is the bottleneck.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** Standard PyTorch execution memory scales linearly with total samples ($Rays \times Samples/Ray$). At 1024x1024 resolution with 512 samples, standard PyTorch evaluation memory is ~12.4 GB [S1, unofficial]. The Triton kernel reduces peak VRAM usage to a near-constant footprint below 430 MiB, achieving a 99.5% memory reduction at high workloads [S1, unofficial]. At 1024x1024 with 512 samples, Triton evaluation memory is 110.7 MiB, and training memory is 121.6 MiB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Time:** Standard PyTorch execution time degrades super-linearly beyond 1024x1024 resolution due to memory bandwidth limits and cache thrashing [S1, unofficial]. Triton maintains predictable linear scaling proportional to total rays rendered [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   At 2048x2048 resolution with 256 ray samples, the Triton kernel reduces iteration time from 1,804.32 ms to 9.16 ms, a 197x speedup compared to the non-Triton version [S1, unofficial].
-*   At 1024x1024 resolution with 512 samples, non-Triton evaluation time is 82.0 ms, while Triton training step time is 15.4 ms [S1, unofficial].
-*   Triton training adds ~3.05x overhead compared to its own evaluation, while standard PyTorch adds ~1.31x overhead [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Massive memory reduction, significant speedup at high resolutions, predictable scaling.
-*   **Limitations:** Nondeterministic gradients due to atomic operations, requires Triton compiler support.
-
-**When to Use:**
-Use when rendering high-resolution volumes (e.g., 2048x2048 or larger) where standard PyTorch implementations hit memory bandwidth limits. Avoid when strict deterministic gradients are required without tolerance adjustments.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Triton maintains predictable linear scaling proportional to total rays rendered [S1, unofficial]. Standard PyTorch execution time degrades super-linearly beyond 1024x1024 resolution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Peak VRAM usage scales to a near-constant footprint below 430 MiB, achieving a 99.5% memory reduction at high workloads compared to standard PyTorch which scales linearly with total samples (Rays × Samples/Ray) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   At 2048x2048 resolution with 256 samples: Triton iteration time is 9.16 ms vs. 1,804.32 ms for standard PyTorch (197x speedup) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   At 1024x1024 resolution with 512 samples: Triton evaluation memory is 110.7 MiB vs. ~12.4 GB for standard PyTorch [S1, unofficial].
+    *   At 1024x1024 resolution with 512 samples: Triton training step time is 15.4 ms [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Significant reduction in memory footprint and iteration time at high resolutions; avoids cache thrashing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Order-nondeterminism in gradients requires adjusted tolerance settings; requires Triton runtime support [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the FlashDRR project, specifically the implementation of a fused CUDA/Triton kernel for volume raycasting [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the performance bottlenecks of standard PyTorch-based volume rendering, particularly in the context of differentiable rendering and large-scale medical imaging applications. The approach leverages the Triton programming model to fuse operations and minimize memory transfers [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the FlashDRR project, which implements a fused GPU rendering technique using Triton to perform volume raycasting [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the memory bandwidth limits and cache thrashing issues inherent in standard PyTorch-based volume rendering implementations at high resolutions [S1, unofficial].
 
